{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8dbe4d35008d3a31", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "A certificate of deposit (CD) is a savings account that holds a fixed amount of money for a fixed period of time, such as six months, one year, or five years, and in exchange, the issuing bank pays interest.", "question": null, "answer": "A certificate of deposit (CD) holds a set amount of money for a set period, such as six months or one year. In return, the issuing bank pays interest. When you redeem the CD, you receive your original deposit plus any interest. Check its terms for the maturity date and any penalty for withdrawing the money early.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1203, "output_tokens": 222}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8dbe4d35008d3a31", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "When you cash in or redeem your CD, you receive the money you originally invested plus any interest.", "question": "What do you receive when you redeem a certificate of deposit (CD)?", "answer": "You receive the money you originally invested plus any interest earned.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1203, "output_tokens": 136}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8dbe4d35008d3a31", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The risk with CDs is the risk that inflation will grow faster than your money, and lower your real returns over time.", "question": "Misconception: Because CDs are considered one of the safest savings options, they have no meaningful risks.", "answer": "CDs can still have inflation risk. If inflation grows faster than your money, it can lower your real returns over time. Being considered a safe savings option does not mean a CD is risk-free.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1203, "output_tokens": 356}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0e43dc002e9e322e", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "CD Interest Rates and Maturity", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The disclosure statement should outline the interest rate on the CD and say if the rate is fixed or variable.", "question": null, "answer": "A CD holds money for a fixed period, and its disclosure statement should identify the interest rate and whether it is fixed or variable. It should also state the CD’s maturity date—the date the term ends—and when interest is paid. When you redeem the CD, you receive your original deposit plus any interest.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1203, "output_tokens": 186}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0e43dc002e9e322e", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "CD Interest Rates and Maturity", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The disclosure statement should outline the interest rate on the CD and say if the rate is fixed or variable.", "question": "What should a CD disclosure statement tell you about its interest rate, and where can you find the CD’s maturity date?", "answer": "It should state the CD’s interest rate and whether that rate is fixed or variable. It should also clearly state the maturity date, which tells you when the CD’s fixed term ends.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1203, "output_tokens": 171}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0e43dc002e9e322e", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "CD Interest Rates and Maturity", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The disclosure statement should outline the interest rate on the CD and say if the rate is fixed or variable.", "question": "Misconception: A CD’s interest rate is always fixed until maturity, so there’s no need to check whether the rate is fixed or variable. What’s the correction?", "answer": "A CD’s rate may be fixed or variable. Check the disclosure statement to see which applies, and note the CD’s maturity date and any early-withdrawal penalties.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1203, "output_tokens": 224}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:97a754cd6cd614ea", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "FDIC Insurance for CDs", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "A CD bought through a federally insured bank is insured up to $250,000. The $250,000 insurance covers all accounts in your name at the same bank, not each CD or account you have at the bank.", "question": null, "answer": "A CD bought through a federally insured bank is insured up to $250,000. This limit applies to all accounts in your name at that same bank combined—it is not a separate $250,000 limit for each CD or account.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1202, "output_tokens": 204}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:97a754cd6cd614ea", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "FDIC Insurance for CDs", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The $250,000 insurance covers all accounts in your name at the same bank, not each CD or account you have at the bank.", "question": "How does the $250,000 insurance limit apply to CDs at the same bank?", "answer": "It applies to all accounts in your name at that bank combined, rather than providing a separate $250,000 limit for each CD or account.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1202, "output_tokens": 219}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:97a754cd6cd614ea", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "FDIC Insurance for CDs", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The $250,000 insurance covers all accounts in your name at the same bank, not each CD or account you have at the bank.", "question": "Misconception: FDIC insurance gives you a separate $250,000 limit for every CD you hold at the same bank.", "answer": "Correction: The source says that the $250,000 insurance covers all accounts in your name at the same bank, rather than applying separately to each CD or account.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1202, "output_tokens": 248}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c3fbf8182798d8c7", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Early Withdrawal Penalties", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The maturity date should be clearly stated, as should any penalties for the “early withdrawal” of the money in the CD.", "question": null, "answer": "A CD has a stated maturity date, and its disclosure should also explain any penalty for withdrawing the money early. Before opening or withdrawing from a CD, review those terms so you know what penalty may apply; the source does not specify a standard penalty amount or method.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1201, "output_tokens": 203}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c3fbf8182798d8c7", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Early Withdrawal Penalties", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The maturity date should be clearly stated, as should any penalties for the “early withdrawal” of the money in the CD.", "question": "What should a CD’s disclosure statement say about withdrawing money before the CD matures?", "answer": "It should state any penalties for early withdrawal, so you can understand the possible cost of taking the money out before the maturity date.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1201, "output_tokens": 161}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c3fbf8182798d8c7", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Early Withdrawal Penalties", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The maturity date should be clearly stated, as should any penalties for the “early withdrawal” of the money in the CD.", "question": "Misconception: You can always withdraw money from a CD before maturity without a penalty.", "answer": "Check the CD’s disclosure statement: it should state any penalties for early withdrawal, so you should not assume an early withdrawal is penalty-free.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1201, "output_tokens": 219}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ac7b00c0bb85b50a", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Inflation Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The risk with CDs is the risk that inflation will grow faster than your money, and lower your real returns over time.", "question": null, "answer": "Inflation risk is the chance that prices rise faster than the money in your CD grows. If that happens, your real return—the return after accounting for inflation—will be lower over time, even though the CD earns interest.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1199, "output_tokens": 237}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ac7b00c0bb85b50a", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Inflation Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The risk with CDs is the risk that inflation will grow faster than your money, and lower your real returns over time.", "question": "What is the inflation risk associated with a CD?", "answer": "Inflation may grow faster than the money in your CD, lowering your real returns over time.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1199, "output_tokens": 197}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ac7b00c0bb85b50a", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Inflation Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The risk with CDs is the risk that inflation will grow faster than your money, and lower your real returns over time.", "question": "Misconception: A CD pays interest, so its purchasing power is guaranteed to increase over time. What is the correction?", "answer": "A CD’s interest does not guarantee that its purchasing power will increase. Inflation can grow faster than the money in the CD, lowering its real returns over time.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1199, "output_tokens": 181}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:02723f8c307c7d99", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Brokered CDs", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "These individuals and entities, known as “deposit brokers,” can sometimes negotiate a higher rate of interest for a CD by promising to bring a certain amount of deposits to the institution.", "question": null, "answer": "A brokered CD is a certificate of deposit offered to customers through a brokerage firm or an independent salesperson called a deposit broker, rather than purchased directly from a bank. A deposit broker may negotiate a higher interest rate by promising to bring a certain amount of deposits to the issuing institution. Before considering one, check the background of both the issuer and the broker: deposit brokers are not licensed or certified, and no state or federal agency approves them.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1200, "output_tokens": 259}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:02723f8c307c7d99", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Brokered CDs", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The deposit broker can then offer these “brokered CDs” to their customers.", "question": "What is a brokered CD?", "answer": "A brokered CD is a certificate of deposit that a deposit broker offers to customers. The broker may negotiate a higher interest rate by promising to bring a certain amount of deposits to the issuing institution.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1200, "output_tokens": 155}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:02723f8c307c7d99", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Brokered CDs", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Deposit brokers are not licensed or certified, and no state or federal agency approves them.", "question": "Misconception: If a broker offers a brokered CD, that broker must be licensed or approved by a government agency. Is this true?", "answer": "No. Deposit brokers are not licensed or certified, and no state or federal agency approves them. Check the background of both the broker and the institution issuing the CD.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1200, "output_tokens": 264}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:28b71c29cf924f5a", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Researching Deposit Brokers", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Since anyone can claim to be a deposit broker, always check whether the deposit broker or the company he or she works for has a history of complaints or fraud.", "question": null, "answer": "When researching a deposit broker, check the background of both the broker and the institution issuing the CD. Deposit brokers are not licensed or certified, and no state or federal agency approves them. Look for a history of complaints or fraud. If the broker is affiliated with an investment professional, the source recommends checking the SEC’s and FINRA’s online databases; your state securities regulator may also have information. For a broker not affiliated with an investment firm, start with your state’s consumer protection office.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1201, "output_tokens": 255}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:28b71c29cf924f5a", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Researching Deposit Brokers", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "To research the background of deposit brokers who are not affiliated with an investment firm, start by contacting your state’s consumer protection office.", "question": "How can you research a deposit broker’s background before buying a brokered CD?", "answer": "Check whether the broker or their company has a history of complaints or fraud. If the broker is affiliated with an investment professional, you can look up disciplinary history in the SEC’s and FINRA’s online databases and contact your state securities regulator for additional information. If the broker is not affiliated with an investment firm, start by contacting your state’s consumer protection office.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1201, "output_tokens": 222}}
{"id": "b69cf98618ccee32:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:28b71c29cf924f5a", "source_id": "b69cf98618ccee32", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/certificates-deposit-cds", "topic": "Certificates of Deposit (CDs)", "subtopic": "Researching Deposit Brokers", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Deposit brokers are not licensed or certified, and no state or federal agency approves them.", "question": "Misconception: A person offering a brokered CD must be licensed or approved by a government agency, so there is no need to research them. What is the correction?", "answer": "Deposit brokers are not licensed or certified, and no state or federal agency approves them. Research the broker’s and issuer’s background, including any history of complaints or fraud. If the broker is affiliated with an investment professional, SEC and FINRA databases may provide disciplinary-history information; for brokers not affiliated with an investment firm, start with your state’s consumer protection office.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1201, "output_tokens": 232}}
